IMPORT prelude

-- Cybersecurity Act 2018 (Singapore) -- who needs a cybersecurity service provider's licence and what follows from not having one
-- (s 2 "cybersecurity service", Second Schedule, Part 5: ss 24, 26, 28 to 33, 35); what the Commissioner may demand of any person or
-- computer owner when investigating an incident (Part 4: ss 19, 20); the incident-reporting, audit and risk-assessment duties of an
-- owner of provider-owned critical information infrastructure (ss 14 to 16); and the maximum penalties for those offences.
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition ("incorporates all amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on
-- 31 December 2021"), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/CA2018.txt, retrieved 1 October 2026 as the current version at
-- that date. The latest amendment annotated is Act 19 of 2024, with effect from 31 October 2025 (136 annotations; three items,
-- among them ss 11 and 18, deleted by it). The arrangement of sections at the top of the deposit is one number out of step with the
-- body near the end (it lists "48. Saving and transitional provisions"; the body numbers that section 49); the body is followed.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0065 in ../../../requirements.jsonl: Tier 2 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. It asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a sharper
-- question yet. SCOPED to what a business or individual most often meets: the licensing of penetration testers and managed SOC
-- providers, the Commissioner's investigation powers over anyone, and the core duties of a critical information infrastructure (CII)
-- owner. Not encoded: designation of CII (ss 7 to 13), the Part 3A, 3B, 3C and 3D regimes (third-party-owned CII, systems of temporary
-- cybersecurity concern, and the 2024 additions), the content of s 14's prescribed periods (they are in regulations not retrieved),
-- emergency measures (s 23), codes of practice, the general offences and procedure in Part 6, and the transitional s 49.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill conventions. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cybersecurity Act 2018 -- services and incidents`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

§§ `2 and Second Schedule -- cybersecurity services, and which are licensable`

-- s 2(1): "cybersecurity service" means "a service provided by a person for reward that is intended primarily for or aimed at ensuring
-- or safeguarding the cybersecurity of a computer or computer system belonging to another person (A)", and includes (a) testing by
-- "searching for vulnerabilities in, and compromising" A's defences; (b) forensic examination; (c) investigating and responding to an
-- incident; (d) a thorough examination to detect threats that "may have evaded detection"; (e) "designing, selling, importing,
-- exporting, installing, maintaining, repairing or servicing" cybersecurity solutions.
-- s 2(2): a person does NOT provide a cybersecurity service only because the person (a) sells, or sells licences for, programs "intended
-- to be installed by a user without the assistance of the seller"; or (b) manages a network or system to ensure "availability" or
-- enhance "performance".
-- Second Schedule para 1: the licensable cybersecurity services are only "(a) managed security operations centre (SOC) monitoring
-- service; (b) penetration testing service". Para 2: penetration testing includes "utilising social engineering".
DECLARE Activity IS ONE OF
    `penetration testing of a client's systems`
    `social engineering tests of a client's staff`
    `managed SOC monitoring of a client's systems`
    `forensic examination of a client's computer`
    `incident response for a client`
    `threat hunting in a client's systems`
    `installing firewalls for a client`
    `selling self-install antivirus licences`
    `managing a client's network for availability and performance`

GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity, `done for reward` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the activity is a cybersecurity service` IF
        `done for reward`
    AND CONSIDER `the activity`
        WHEN `selling self-install antivirus licences` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `managing a client's network for availability and performance` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity, `done for reward` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the activity is a licensable cybersecurity service` IF
        `the activity is a cybersecurity service` `the activity` `done for reward`
    AND CONSIDER `the activity`
        WHEN `penetration testing of a client's systems` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `social engineering tests of a client's staff` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `managed SOC monitoring of a client's systems` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `24 -- no licensable service without a licence`

-- s 24(1): except under a licence granted or renewed under s 26, no person (a) "may engage in the business of providing any licensable
-- cybersecurity service to other persons"; or (b) being in that business, may "advertise, or in any way hold out" that it provides it.
-- (2): "a fine not exceeding $50,000 or ... imprisonment for a term not exceeding 2 years or ... both". (3): (1) "does not apply to the
-- provision of a cybersecurity service by a company to its related company" (Companies Act 1967, s 6).
GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity, `done for reward` IS A BOOLEAN, `in the business of providing it or holding out` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `holds a licence` IS A BOOLEAN, `the client is a related company` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `providing the activity is an offence under s 24` IF
        `the activity is a licensable cybersecurity service` `the activity` `done for reward`
    AND `in the business of providing it or holding out`
    AND (NOT `holds a licence`)
    AND (NOT `the client is a related company`)

§§ `31 -- no fee recoverable in court without a licence`

-- s 31: "Any person who provides any licensable cybersecurity service is not entitled to bring any proceeding in any court to recover
-- any commission, fee, gain or reward for the service provided unless, at the time of providing the service, the person is the holder
-- of a valid cybersecurity service provider's licence." s 31 has no related-company exception of its own.
GIVEN `the activity` IS AN Activity, `held a valid licence when providing it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the provider may sue for the fee` IF
        (NOT `the activity is a licensable cybersecurity service` `the activity` TRUE)
    OR  `held a valid licence when providing it`

§§ `26 and 28 -- renewal and the life of a licence`

-- s 26(1)(c): a renewal application must be "made not later than one month or any other period before the expiry of the licence
-- (called in this section the renewal period) that may be prescribed". (6): "Where a person submits an application for the renewal of
-- the person's licence before the start of the renewal period, the licence continues in force until the date on which the licence is
-- renewed or the application for its renewal is refused". The renewal period is counted here in days (one month is not a fixed number
-- of days; the cases use 30). Read literally, an application made exactly at the start of the renewal period is in time under (1)(c)
-- but not "before the start" under (6) -- that edge is a reading, not a ruling.
-- s 28(2), (3): a licence, and a renewal, is in force for a period "not exceeding 5 years".
GIVEN `days before expiry the application was made` IS A NUMBER, `renewal period in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the renewal application is in time` IF `days before expiry the application was made` AT LEAST `renewal period in days`

GIVEN `days before expiry the application was made` IS A NUMBER, `renewal period in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the licence continues until the application is decided` IF
    `days before expiry the application was made` GREATER THAN `renewal period in days`

GIVEN `years specified in the licence` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the licence period is within s 28` IF `years specified in the licence` AT MOST 5

§§ `29 -- records`

-- s 29(1): for each engagement the licensee keeps the client's name and address, who provided the service, the date and the type of
-- service, and must "(b) retain every such record for a period of not less than 3 years after the date of the occasion".
-- (3): knowingly making a false or misleading record and furnishing it on a requirement under (2) -- $10,000 or 12 months.
-- (4): (3) "does not apply if the record is not false or misleading in a material particular".
GIVEN `years since the engagement` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the record may be discarded` IF `years since the engagement` AT LEAST 3

GIVEN `knowingly made a false or misleading record` IS A BOOLEAN, `furnished it on the licensing officer's requirement` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `false or misleading in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the false record is an offence` IF
        `knowingly made a false or misleading record`
    AND `furnished it on the licensing officer's requirement`
    AND `false or misleading in a material particular`

§§ `30 -- revocation and suspension`

-- s 30(2)(a): suspension "for a period not exceeding 6 months". (4): no revocation or suspension within 14 days of informing the
-- licensee of the intention "unless the licensee concerned is given an opportunity to be heard". (6): a revocation or suspension order
-- "(a) takes effect immediately upon service of the notice ... where the licensing officer states in the order that it is undesirable
-- in the public interest for the licensee to continue"; "(b) in any other case, takes effect at the end of 14 days after the service".
GIVEN `the order states it is undesirable in the public interest to continue` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days after service until the order takes effect` MEANS
    IF `the order states it is undesirable in the public interest to continue` THEN 0 ELSE 14

GIVEN `months of suspension` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the suspension is within s 30` IF `months of suspension` AT MOST 6

§§ `32 and 33 -- financial penalties`

-- s 32(1): for a contravention of Part 5 that is not an offence, or a breach of a licence condition. (2): a financial penalty "not
-- exceeding $10,000 for each contravention or failure to comply, but not exceeding in the aggregate $50,000".
-- s 33(3): the date on which the order is intended to be made "must not be earlier than 21 days after the date of the written notice".
GIVEN `number of contraventions` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum financial penalty` MEANS `the lesser of` (`number of contraventions` TIMES 10000) 50000

GIVEN `days between notice and intended order` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the penalty order date is permissible` IF `days between notice and intended order` AT LEAST 21

§§ `35 -- appeal to the Minister`

-- s 35(1) to (4): appeal against a refusal, conditions, an order under s 30 or a financial penalty "within the relevant period";
-- (8): "relevant period" means "14 days or such longer period as the Minister allows ... whether allowed before or after the end of
-- the 14 days". (6): an appeal "(except an order mentioned in section 30(6)(b) or 32(2)) does not affect the effect of the decision
-- appealed against ... and the decision appealed against must be complied with until the determination of the appeal". (7): the
-- Minister's decision "is final".
DECLARE Decision IS ONE OF
    `refusal of a licence or renewal`
    `licence conditions`
    `revocation or suspension stated to be in the public interest`
    `revocation or suspension taking effect after 14 days`
    `a financial penalty order`

GIVEN `days after notification` IS A NUMBER, `longer period allowed by the Minister` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the appeal is in time` IF
        `days after notification` AT MOST 14
    OR  `days after notification` AT MOST `longer period allowed by the Minister`

GIVEN `the decision` IS A Decision
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the decision must be complied with pending the appeal` IF
    CONSIDER `the decision`
    WHEN `revocation or suspension taking effect after 14 days` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a financial penalty order` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `19 and 20 -- investigating incidents`

-- s 19(2): on information about any threat or incident, an incident response officer may require "any person" to attend and answer,
-- or produce records. (6): no one is obliged to disclose information subject to "any right, privilege or immunity conferred, or
-- obligation or limitation imposed, by or under any law or rules of professional conduct", "except that the performance of a
-- contractual obligation is not an excuse for not disclosing the information". (7): disclosing in good faith is no breach of contract.
-- (8): $5,000 or 6 months.
-- s 20(3): the wider powers (remedial directions, entry, scanning, seizure) need a threat or incident that (a) risks significant harm to
-- provider-owned CII; (b) risks disruption of an essential service; (c) threatens "national security, defence, foreign relations,
-- economy, public health, public safety or public order"; or (d) is "of a severe nature" by harm, number of computers or value of
-- information at risk, "whether or not" the computers are CII.
-- s 20(2)(h): take possession of a computer "with the owner's consent"; (5) without consent only if the Commissioner is satisfied it is
-- (a) necessary, (b) "there is no less disruptive method", (c) after consultation the benefit outweighs the detriment, "and the
-- Commissioner has issued ... a written authorisation". (7): $25,000 or 2 years.
DECLARE Reason IS ONE OF
    `legal professional privilege`
    `a statutory secrecy obligation`
    `a confidentiality clause in a contract`
    `commercial inconvenience`

GIVEN `the reason` IS A Reason
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may withhold the information` IF
    CONSIDER `the reason`
    WHEN `legal professional privilege` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a statutory secrecy obligation` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `risk of significant harm to CII` IS A BOOLEAN, `risk of disruption to an essential service` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `threat to national security, economy, public health, safety or order` IS A BOOLEAN, `severe by harm, scale or value` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the severity threshold is met` IF
        `risk of significant harm to CII`
    OR  `risk of disruption to an essential service`
    OR  `threat to national security, economy, public health, safety or order`
    OR  `severe by harm, scale or value`

GIVEN `the owner consents` IS A BOOLEAN, `necessary for the investigation` IS A BOOLEAN, `no less disruptive method` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `benefit outweighs detriment after consultation` IS A BOOLEAN, `written authorisation issued` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may take possession of the computer` IF
        `the owner consents`
    OR  (    `necessary for the investigation`
         AND `no less disruptive method`
         AND `benefit outweighs detriment after consultation`
         AND `written authorisation issued`)

§§ `14 to 16 -- duties of an owner of provider-owned critical information infrastructure`

-- s 14(1): notify the Commissioner of prescribed incidents in the CII, in other systems under the owner's control (whether or not
-- interconnected: paras (b), (ba)), and in a supplier's systems interconnected with the CII (para (bb)), "within the prescribed period
-- after becoming aware". (3): $100,000 or 2 years.
-- s 15(1): an audit by an approved auditor "at least once every 2 years (or at such higher frequency as may be directed)", and a risk
-- assessment "at least once a year", both from the s 7 notice. (2): a copy of the report "not later than 30 days after the completion".
-- (7): failing (1) -- $100,000 or 2 years "and, in the case of a continuing offence, to a further fine not exceeding $5,000 for every day
-- or part of a day during which the offence continues after conviction". (8): failing (2) -- $25,000 or 12 months, and $2,500 a day.
-- s 16(3): failing to take part in a directed cybersecurity exercise -- "a fine not exceeding $100,000" (no imprisonment).
DECLARE Incident IS ONE OF
    `an incident in the CII itself`
    `an incident in an interconnected system the owner controls`
    `an incident in a stand-alone system the owner controls`
    `an incident in a supplier's interconnected system`
    `an incident in a supplier's stand-alone system`

GIVEN `the incident` IS AN Incident, `of a prescribed kind` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the owner must notify the Commissioner` IF
        `of a prescribed kind`
    AND CONSIDER `the incident`
        WHEN `an incident in a supplier's stand-alone system` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `months since the last audit` IS A NUMBER, `months since the last risk assessment` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the owner is behind on s 15(1)` IF
        `months since the last audit` GREATER THAN 24
    OR  `months since the last risk assessment` GREATER THAN 12

GIVEN `days after completion` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the report was furnished in time` IF `days after completion` AT MOST 30

GIVEN `days the failure continued after conviction` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for not furnishing the report` MEANS 25000 PLUS 2500 TIMES `days the failure continued after conviction`

GIVEN `days the failure continued after conviction` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for not auditing or assessing` MEANS 100000 PLUS 5000 TIMES `days the failure continued after conviction`

§§ `Maximum penalties for the offences in scope`

-- As quoted above: s 24(2) $50,000 / 2 years; s 26(7) false licence application $10,000 / 12 months; s 29(3) $10,000 / 12 months;
-- s 29A(3) obstructing the licensing officer's monitoring "without reasonable excuse" $10,000 / 12 months; s 19(8) $5,000 / 6 months;
-- s 20(7) $25,000 / 2 years; s 14(3) $100,000 / 2 years; s 15(7) $100,000 / 2 years; s 15(8) $25,000 / 12 months; s 16(3) $100,000
-- and no term of imprisonment. Continuing-offence daily fines are above.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `unlicensed provision or holding out`
    `false statement in a licence application`
    `false record furnished to the licensing officer`
    `failing a monitoring requirement`
    `refusing an incident response officer under s 19`
    `defying a direction under s 20`
    `CII owner failing to notify an incident`
    `CII owner failing to audit or assess`
    `CII owner failing to furnish a report`
    `CII owner skipping a directed exercise`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `unlicensed provision or holding out` THEN 50000
    WHEN `false statement in a licence application` THEN 10000
    WHEN `false record furnished to the licensing officer` THEN 10000
    WHEN `failing a monitoring requirement` THEN 10000
    WHEN `refusing an incident response officer under s 19` THEN 5000
    WHEN `defying a direction under s 20` THEN 25000
    WHEN `CII owner failing to notify an incident` THEN 100000
    WHEN `CII owner failing to audit or assess` THEN 100000
    WHEN `CII owner failing to furnish a report` THEN 25000
    WHEN `CII owner skipping a directed exercise` THEN 100000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `unlicensed provision or holding out` THEN 24
    WHEN `false statement in a licence application` THEN 12
    WHEN `false record furnished to the licensing officer` THEN 12
    WHEN `failing a monitoring requirement` THEN 12
    WHEN `refusing an incident response officer under s 19` THEN 6
    WHEN `defying a direction under s 20` THEN 24
    WHEN `CII owner failing to notify an incident` THEN 24
    WHEN `CII owner failing to audit or assess` THEN 24
    WHEN `CII owner failing to furnish a report` THEN 12
    WHEN `CII owner skipping a directed exercise` THEN 0
